# =============================================================================
# Caddyfile — Two-Layer (user -> R1 -> R2 -> user) LIVE PUBLIC DEMO
# PORT-BASED single-hostname TLS (Azure free *.cloudapp.azure.com compatible)
# =============================================================================
#
# Purpose: defeat the browser MIXED-CONTENT block. The prod client is served over
# HTTPS, and an HTTPS page makes the browser BLOCK any ws:// or http:// sub-resource.
# So EVERY browser-facing endpoint the client touches must be TLS.
#
# WHY PORTS, NOT SUBDOMAINS (the Azure free-DNS constraint):
# Azure's free `*.cloudapp.azure.com` gives EXACTLY ONE A-record per public IP —
# `<label>.<region>.cloudapp.azure.com` — with NO wildcard and NO sub-label ability.
# So `app.<dns>` / `rpc.<dns>` / `sig.<dns>` do NOT resolve and Let's Encrypt CANNOT
# issue certs for them. The prior 4-subdomain scheme was therefore un-deployable.
# This rewrite fronts ONE hostname per VM and separates the backends by distinct
# TLS PORTS. Caddy provisions ONE cert per hostname and REUSES it across all its
# ports (443 / 8443 / 9443 / 7443), so a single ACME issuance covers the whole VM.
#
# WHY PORTS AND NOT PATH-ROUTING: ports preserve each backend's ROOT path. Sui RPC
# serves JSON-RPC at `/`, and the relay/signaling WS servers upgrade at `/` (and the
# relay serves /api/probe, /healthz off its own port) — a `handle_path /rpc/*` prefix
# strip would risk a root-path or WS-upgrade-path mismatch. One backend = one port =
# zero path rewriting. (Verified against the client: it opens `new WebSocket(url)` and
# feeds VITE_SUI_RPC_URL straight into dapp-kit `createNetworkConfig({localnet:{url}})`
# with NO path append and NO hard-coded port — so a `wss://host:8443` URL works as-is.)
#
#   Browser touches              ->  TLS front (Caddy, one hostname)      ->  daemon (loopback)
#   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   client static app (SPA)          https://dvconf-kr...:443              file_server dist/
#   Sui RPC (JSON-RPC at /)          https://dvconf-kr...:7443             localhost:9000
#   signaling WS (cap-token)         wss://dvconf-kr...:9443               localhost:8080
#   relay-KR control WS (R1)         wss://dvconf-kr...:8443               localhost:4000
#   relay-JP control WS (R2)         wss://dvconf-jp...:443                localhost:4000  (JP VM)
#
# NOT PROXIED — media (RTP/RTCP) stays DIRECT UDP to each relay's ANNOUNCED_IP
# (udp 40000-49999). DTLS/SRTP already encrypts the media, so it needs no TLS front
# and MUST NOT go through Caddy (Caddy is HTTP/WS/TCP only, not a UDP media proxy).
# The browser reaches media by the ICE candidates the relay advertises
# (ANNOUNCED_IP = each VM's raw public IP), NEVER through a *.cloudapp.azure.com name.
#
# CRITICAL — the on-chain relay endpoint URLs MUST be these wss:// port URLs:
# The client consumes each relay's CONTROL endpoint URL as delivered by on-chain
# `RoomAssigned` (relay_registry endpoint_url, read verbatim in useRoomAssignment.ts).
# If those are registered as `ws://<pub-ip>:4000` the browser mixed-content-BLOCKS them.
# So register (via wan-bootstrap / lane-b assignment):
#     relay-KR endpoint_url = wss://dvconf-kr.koreacentral.cloudapp.azure.com:8443
#     relay-JP endpoint_url = wss://dvconf-jp.japaneast.cloudapp.azure.com   (:443)
# (ANNOUNCED_IP for MEDIA stays the raw public IP — only the CONTROL URL becomes wss.)
#
# TEMPLATING: the two decided Azure DNS names are HARDCODED below (they are fixed for
# this demo). If you re-provision with different labels, change these two hostnames:
#     KR VM  = dvconf-kr.koreacentral.cloudapp.azure.com
#     JP VM  = dvconf-jp.japaneast.cloudapp.azure.com
# On each VM keep ONLY that VM's block (delete the other) — or use a per-VM copy.
#
# PORTS THE NSG/UFW MUST OPEN (inbound TCP): 80 + 443 (ACME HTTP-01 challenge + the
# app/JP-relay) AND 8443 + 9443 + 7443 (the extra KR TLS ports). Keep 80 AND 443 open
# on BOTH VMs even though services also sit on 8443/9443/7443 — the ACME challenge and
# cert renewal ride 80/443. See azure-wan-runbook.md Section 2 (updated port list).
#
# NOTE: syntax is Caddy v2, hand-written; NOT validated with `caddy validate` here
# (no live env). Run `caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile` on the VM first.
# =============================================================================


# =====================================================================
# ===================== KR VM (koreacentral) ==========================
#   R1 (primary relay, ALIVE) + committee (cp / signaling / validators)
#   + Sui localnet + the built client static app.
#   ONE hostname (dvconf-kr...), FOUR TLS ports (443 / 8443 / 9443 / 7443).
#   Keep this block on the KR VM; delete the JP block below.
# =====================================================================

# --- client static app (:443): file_server on the built Vite dist/ ---
# The DEFAULT HTTPS port. This is the URL the human opens in the browser.
# Replace the root path with wherever you scp'd the built dist/ (placeholder).
dvconf-kr.koreacentral.cloudapp.azure.com {
	root * /var/www/dvconf-dist      # <-- PLACEHOLDER: the scp'd client `dist/`
	encode zstd gzip
	file_server
	# SPA fallback: unknown paths serve index.html (client-side routing).
	try_files {path} /index.html
}

# --- relay-KR control WS (R1 primary) on :8443 -> local relay WS_PORT :4000 ---
# The client HOMES here for control/signaling (produce/consume/pause). This exact
# `wss://dvconf-kr.koreacentral.cloudapp.azure.com:8443` URL is what the KR relay
# registers on-chain as its endpoint_url (see the CRITICAL note in the header).
# Media is NOT here — it is direct UDP to ANNOUNCED_IP:40000-49999.
# Caddy reverse_proxy upgrades WebSocket transparently (no special directive in v2).
dvconf-kr.koreacentral.cloudapp.azure.com:8443 {
	reverse_proxy localhost:4000
}

# --- signaling WS (cap-token join) on :9443 -> local signaling :8080 ---
# The client opens VITE_SIGNALING_URL=wss://dvconf-kr...:9443 for the cap-token
# admission / signaling channel.
dvconf-kr.koreacentral.cloudapp.azure.com:9443 {
	reverse_proxy localhost:8080
}

# --- Sui RPC on :7443 -> local full-node JSON-RPC :9000 ---
# The client reads VITE_SUI_RPC_URL=https://dvconf-kr...:7443; the room list + all
# on-chain reads load from here. JSON-RPC is served at the ROOT path `/`, which the
# port scheme preserves (no prefix strip).
dvconf-kr.koreacentral.cloudapp.azure.com:7443 {
	reverse_proxy localhost:9000
}

# --- OPTIONAL: 5 daemon /healthz + canary routes (dashboard liveness widgets) ---
# These power the client's DaemonHealthPanel / canary-coverage widgets. They are
# OPTIONAL: if omitted, those widgets render DEGRADED (unknown/down) but the demo
# (create room / join / media) is UNAFFECTED. To light them up over TLS, give each
# its OWN distinct TLS port on the same hostname and build the client with the matching
# VITE_*_HEALTHZ_URL (e.g. https://dvconf-kr...:6443). Daemon loopback ports:
# cp 8091 / sig 8082 / val 8101 / relay 4001 (METRICS_PORT) / canary 8102. Each extra
# TLS port must ALSO be opened in the NSG/UFW. Leave commented to accept degraded widgets.
#
# dvconf-kr.koreacentral.cloudapp.azure.com:6443 { reverse_proxy localhost:8091 }  # VITE_CP_HEALTHZ_URL
# dvconf-kr.koreacentral.cloudapp.azure.com:6444 { reverse_proxy localhost:8082 }  # VITE_SIGNALING_HEALTHZ_URL
# dvconf-kr.koreacentral.cloudapp.azure.com:6445 { reverse_proxy localhost:8101 }  # VITE_VALIDATOR_HEALTHZ_URL
# dvconf-kr.koreacentral.cloudapp.azure.com:6446 { reverse_proxy localhost:4001 }  # VITE_RELAY_HEALTHZ_URL (relay METRICS_PORT)
# dvconf-kr.koreacentral.cloudapp.azure.com:6447 { reverse_proxy localhost:8102 }  # VITE_VALIDATOR_CANARY_COVERAGE_URL


# =====================================================================
# ======================= JP VM (japaneast) ===========================
#   R2 (standby relay, active-forwards R1's media over the WAN pipe).
#   Keep ONLY this block on the JP VM; delete the KR block above.
#   The JP VM fronts JUST the relay control WS — no app / rpc / signaling
#   (those all live on the KR VM). ONE hostname, ONE TLS port (:443).
# =====================================================================

# --- relay-JP control WS (R2 standby) on :443 -> local relay WS_PORT :4000 ---
# A demo/failover browser pins to R2 via ?relayPin=standby (or a full-URL pin); the
# client opens this wss for control. Because the JP VM has only ONE service, it uses
# the default HTTPS port 443 (no extra ports needed). This exact
# `wss://dvconf-jp.japaneast.cloudapp.azure.com` (:443) URL is what the JP relay
# registers on-chain as its endpoint_url. Media for the pinned browser is direct UDP
# to the JP VM's ANNOUNCED_IP:40000-49999.
dvconf-jp.japaneast.cloudapp.azure.com {
	reverse_proxy localhost:4000
}
